      title: "A Shi'i Century",
      body: `## Shi'i rulers, Sunni caliph

In the middle of the tenth century, much of the Islamic world was ruled by Shi'is. In Baghdad, as we saw, the **Buyids** held the Sunni Abbasid caliph as their pensioner from 945. They were Twelver Shi'is from Daylam, and under them the public rituals of Shi'ism took shape: in 963 Mu'izz al-Dawla ordered the markets of Baghdad closed on the day of **'Ashura** for mourning the death of Husayn, and the festival of **Ghadir Khumm**, celebrating the Prophet's designation of 'Ali, was publicly observed. Sunni crowds sometimes answered with festivals of their own, and Baghdad's quarters fought one another along sectarian lines.

The greatest of the Buyid rulers, **'Adud al-Dawla** (r. 949–983), united their domains in Iraq and western Iran, patronized scholars and poets, and built in Baghdad a great hospital, the 'Adudi, staffed by some two dozen physicians. Yet the Buyids never abolished the caliphate. A Sunni caliph whom they controlled was more useful to them than a Shi'i one who might claim authority over them.

## The Fatimids

The more dramatic challenge came from the west. The **Isma'ilis**—the branch of the Shi'a that had followed Ja'far al-Sadiq's son Isma'il—had built a secret network of missionaries across the Islamic world, preaching the coming of a Mahdi from the family of the Prophet. In 909 the movement triumphed in **Ifriqiya**, where its missionary had won over the Kutama Berbers. Its leader, **'Ubayd Allah**, was proclaimed caliph with the title **al-Mahdi**. The dynasty took its name from **Fatima**, the Prophet's daughter, from whom it claimed descent.

The Fatimids claimed more than the Abbasids ever had. Their caliph was also the **Imam**, the divinely guided leader of the faithful, possessing knowledge of the hidden, inner meaning of the revelation. Their goal was not to rule a province but to replace the Abbasids as heads of the whole Muslim world.

## Cairo

After several failed attempts, the Fatimids conquered **Egypt** in 969. Their general **Jawhar**, a former slave of Slavic origin, entered Fustat almost without a fight and laid out a new royal city beside it, enclosing palaces and gardens for the caliph and his court. The caliph **al-Mu'izz** arrived in 973. The city was called ***al-Qahira***, "the Victorious"—**Cairo**. Its great mosque, **al-Azhar**, founded in 970, became a center of Isma'ili teaching; centuries later, under the Sunni Mamluks, it would become the most famous Sunni university of the Islamic world.

Fatimid Egypt was rich. It controlled the trade between the Mediterranean and the Red Sea, and at its height ruled North Africa, Sicily, Syria, Palestine, and the holy cities of Arabia. In Cairo, Isma'ili scholars held public sessions of instruction, and the caliphs patronized science; the great scholar **Ibn al-Haytham**, whose *Book of Optics* transformed the understanding of vision, worked in Cairo in the early eleventh century.

The Fatimids were, on the whole, tolerant rulers of a population that remained mostly Sunni and, in large part, Christian. Christians and Jews served at the highest levels of government; the documents of the **Cairo Geniza**, a storeroom of the Ben Ezra synagogue in Fustat, preserve hundreds of thousands of fragments of Jewish letters, contracts, and accounts from these centuries, and are one of the richest sources for everyday life in the medieval Mediterranean. There were exceptions. The caliph **al-Hakim** (r. 996–1021), whose erratic decrees are still debated, persecuted Christians and Jews and in 1009 ordered the destruction of the **Church of the Holy Sepulchre** in Jerusalem. Al-Hakim mysteriously disappeared in 1021; a group that regarded him as divine became the **Druze**.

## The limits of the Shi'i century

The Fatimid caliph **al-Mustansir** reigned for nearly sixty years (1036–1094), the longest reign in Islamic history. In 1058–1059 his name was briefly proclaimed in the Friday prayer in Baghdad itself, when a Turkish general rebelled against the Buyids—the high-water mark of Fatimid ambition. But in the 1060s Egypt suffered famine and military anarchy, and order was restored only by an Armenian general, **Badr al-Jamali**, who became the first of a line of powerful military viziers. When al-Mustansir died in 1094, a succession dispute split the Isma'ilis. The followers of his elder son, **Nizar**, refused to accept the new caliph and broke away; in Iran, under **Hasan-i Sabbah**, who had seized the mountain fortress of **Alamut** in 1090, they formed a state of scattered castles feared for its use of targeted killing. Their enemies called them *hashishiyya*, a term of abuse that reached Europe as **Assassins**.

Meanwhile a new Sunni power had arisen in the east. The Shi'i century was ending.

> **Pause and consider.** The Buyids kept a Sunni caliph they controlled; the Fatimids claimed to be caliphs themselves. What did each dynasty gain from its choice, and what did it risk?`,
